WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.189 --> 00:00:09.860
Noise in Quantum systems refers to the interaction
of the system with the rest of the world.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:16.910
Such interactions are crucial for any sort
of computation and in effect enables classical

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:17.910
computing.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.720
Noise is related to three fundamental topics.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:30.800
The 1st topic is time dependent processes
and thus also to the notion of time in general.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:37.469
A time dependent processes require interaction
with the environment and therefore it requires

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:39.789
some amount of noise.

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:42.460
The second notion is the notion of locality.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:50.960
Locality in quantum systems refers to the
effect quantum processes usually involve between

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.899
a small number of particles.

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:57.969
And it is very important to understand locality.

00:00:57.969 --> 00:01:06.110
According to my analysis quantum computational
supremacy is an artifact of incomplete modeling

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:14.640
of local quantum systems that need to bring
noise and even a substantial amount of noise

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.500
in there definition.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.170
And the third is the connection to computation.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:27.490
It is the interaction of the environment and
noise that enables robust classical information

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.360
and robust classical computation.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:37.520
And it turns out that when the amount of interaction
with the environment is small the quantum

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:44.710
processes are very limited computationally
and very primitive computationally and such

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:53.070
primitive computation power does not enable
computational supremacy and it does not enable

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:58.490
building quantum error correcting codes that
are necessary for quantum computing.

